{"availability":"available","full":"ucb-substrate/argon","name":"argon","description":"A programming language for writing constraint-based integrated circuit layout generators","category":"Developer tools","language":"Rust","stars":17,"forks":2,"created":"2024-06-20T19:46:58Z","updated":"2026-10-16T18:53:38Z","license":"BSD-3-Clause","branch":"main","homepage":"https://ucb-substrate.github.io/argon/","topics":[],"demo":"https://ucb-substrate.github.io/argon/","color":"#63362a","ink":"#f8c7a8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-18T16:53:49.932Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-18T16:53:49.932Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-18T16:53:49.932Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-07T02:54:45.117Z","stars":17,"forks":2},{"at":"2026-10-09T08:56:50.226Z","stars":17,"forks":2},{"at":"2026-10-12T09:43:59.558Z","stars":17,"forks":2},{"at":"2026-10-18T16:53:49.932Z","stars":17,"forks":2}],"technologies":["Rust"],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["Argon is a programming language for writing constraint-based integrated circuit layout generators. Argon's primary feature is bidirectional editing between Neovim and a custom GUI. Simpler geometric constraints can be entered visually in the GUI, while more complex logic can be implemented in code.","Argon's syntax and type system is inspired by Rust. Unlike Rust, Argon is not intended to be a fully featured general-purpose programming language. The main goal of Argon is to allow interoperability with the GUI, enable the creation of most practical parametric cells, and allow for performance optimizations such as caching and incremental compilation."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/ucb-substrate/argon/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"ffba6b173dfb92377c4ae1a194a1c0aa90119b61d5b9d250da0dd002b0ac0484","extractedAt":"2026-10-09T16:45:13.051Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-09T16:45:13.051Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"Argon is a programming language for writing constraint-based integrated circuit layout gene